#: ../../source/guides/engine/conventions/rust.rst:3
msgid "Rust: house style"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/conventions/rust.rst:5
msgid ""
"The engine is one crate, ``packages/freeports``, on edition 2024. ``cargo"
" fmt`` and ``clippy`` settle everything mechanical — ``make fmt`` and "
"``make lint`` — so this page is only about the choices a formatter cannot"
" make for you."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/conventions/rust.rst:11
msgid ""
"Everything below is *a* consistent answer, not *the* answer. Where a "
"module has a reason to differ, differing is fine; what is not fine is "
"differing by accident, because then a reader cannot tell the deviation "
"from the pattern."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/conventions/rust.rst:16
msgid "How the tree is arranged"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/conventions/rust.rst:18
msgid ""
"``src/`` is split by area rather than by kind — ``core``, ``input``, "
"``output``, ``cli``, ``formats_repo``, ``formats_utils``, ``commons``, "
"``python`` — and a module that grows a family of children becomes a "
"directory with a file of the same name beside it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/conventions/rust.rst:22
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"**A parent module is the vocabulary of its area.** It re-exports the "
"types a consumer of that area needs, so callers write ``use "
"crate::core::pipeline::{Extracted, PipeError}`` and never learn how the "
"area happens to be split across files. ``core/pipeline.rs`` is the "
"example worth copying: three ``pub mod`` declarations, then ``pub use`` "
"of everything a caller should see."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/conventions/rust.rst:27
msgid ""
"That is what makes the internal layout free to move. Splitting a file, or"
" merging two, is not a breaking change as long as the parent's re-exports"
" stay put."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/conventions/rust.rst:30
msgid ""
"**The public surface is** ``api``. Everything else is internal, whatever "
"its visibility says. A ``pub`` item deep in the tree is public to the "
"crate's own modules, not a promise to the outside."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/conventions/rust.rst:34
msgid "Doc-comments"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/conventions/rust.rst:36
msgid ""
"``//!`` at the top of every module, ``///`` on every public item. What "
"goes in them is what the code cannot say for itself:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/conventions/rust.rst:39
msgid "what the module or type **guarantees**;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/conventions/rust.rst:40
msgid "**why** it is built this way, where the choice is not obvious;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/conventions/rust.rst:41
msgid "its **known limits**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/conventions/rust.rst:43
msgid ""
"Not: a restatement of the signature, a plan for a future implementer, or "
"a reference to a milestone or a ticket. Those go stale and read as "
"instructions to somebody who is not there."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/conventions/rust.rst:46
msgid ""
"Where a type is non-trivial, add a runnable example. It becomes a doc-"
"test, which means it cannot go stale in silence — the one form of "
"documentation the build can check."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/conventions/rust.rst:49
msgid ""
"The house voice is worth naming, because it is unusual and deliberate: "
"module docs explain the *decision*, often in a sentence that begins with "
"what would have gone wrong otherwise. Read ``core/pipeline.rs`` or "
"``ci/metrics.py`` before writing a long one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/conventions/rust.rst:54
msgid "Errors"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/conventions/rust.rst:56
msgid ""
"**One error enum per module**, named after the module — ``DateError``, "
"``ScheduleError``, ``DocumentError``, ``FreeportsConfigError``. Derived "
"with ``thiserror``, and always ``#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, "
"thiserror::Error)]`` where the payload allows it, so that tests can "
"compare an error to an expected value rather than to a string."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/conventions/rust.rst:61
msgid "**The message names the value that was wrong, and what was expected**::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/conventions/rust.rst:68
msgid ""
"Tuple variants for one value, struct variants as soon as there are two — "
"an error nobody can read at the call site is an error somebody will "
"convert to a string and lose."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/conventions/rust.rst:71
msgid ""
"**A user path does not panic.** ``unwrap`` and ``expect`` are for "
"invariants the type system cannot express and the code has just "
"established, not for \"this should not happen\". Where a panic really is "
"right, ``expect`` with a sentence saying what was assumed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/conventions/rust.rst:76
msgid "Naming"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/conventions/rust.rst:78
msgid "Ordinary Rust naming, with two habits worth knowing:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/conventions/rust.rst:80
msgid ""
"**A newtype rather than a bare** ``String`` where the value has a meaning"
" — ``PipelineName``, ``FormatName``. It costs three lines and removes a "
"class of argument-swap bug."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/conventions/rust.rst:82
msgid ""
"**Builders return** ``Result``, and their error enum is named for the "
"builder: ``RectangleBuildError``, ``LimitsBuildError``. A type that can "
"be constructed wrongly says so in its constructor's signature."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/conventions/rust.rst:87
msgid "Dependencies"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/conventions/rust.rst:89
msgid ""
"Adding one is a decision, and ``Cargo.toml`` is commented like prose for "
"that reason — every non-obvious dependency carries the sentence "
"explaining why *that* one. Two standing choices you will trip over if "
"nobody says them:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/conventions/rust.rst:93
msgid ""
"**Regular expressions use** ``onig`` **(Oniguruma), not the** ``regex`` "
"**crate.** The syntax has to match what a format author writes in a "
"formats repository, which is Python's, and the ``regex`` crate "
"deliberately does not support parts of it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/conventions/rust.rst:96
msgid ""
"**No PyO3** ``extension-module`` **feature.** The crate produces both a "
"Python extension and a binary that *embeds* Python; that feature would "
"break the second. ``Cargo.toml`` explains it at the ``[lib]`` section."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/conventions/rust.rst:100
msgid ""
"Prefer the standard library, then a crate this project already depends "
"on, then a new one. \"It is one function\" is a reason to write the "
"function."
msgstr ""

